1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,949
A blocked heart artery needs help fast.

2
00:00:03,646 --> 00:00:08,754
What happens next? In this example, a
blood clot blocks a coronary

3
00:00:08,812 --> 00:00:13,769
artery. The heart muscle it supplies is
suddenly short of oxygen.

4
00:00:14,466 --> 00:00:17,380
Emergency care can help restore blood
flow.

5
00:00:17,659 --> 00:00:22,152
Warning signs can include chest pressure,
shortness of breath, sweating,

6
00:00:22,303 --> 00:00:26,494
nausea, or discomfort in the arm, back or
jaw.

7
00:00:27,191 --> 00:00:30,047
The symptoms can differ from person to
person.

8
00:00:30,558 --> 00:00:35,596
If a heart attack is suspected, call local
emergency services immediately.

9
00:00:36,107 --> 00:00:40,171
Emergency responders can start assessment
and coordinate hospital care.

10
00:00:40,612 --> 00:00:45,256
The emergency team considers the symptoms
and uses an electrocardiogram, often

11
00:00:45,325 --> 00:00:49,598
called an ECG, to look at the heart’s
electrical activity.

12
00:00:50,294 --> 00:00:54,253
A blood test called troponin can detect
injury to the heart muscle.

13
00:00:54,950 --> 00:00:59,919
An ECG can sometimes identify a pattern
that needs an immediate plan to reopen the

14
00:00:59,988 --> 00:01:00,697
artery.

15
00:01:01,393 --> 00:01:06,072
For a heart attack called STEMI, opening
the artery quickly is a priority.

16
00:01:06,513 --> 00:01:10,855
When a team can perform emergency
angioplasty promptly, it is generally the

17
00:01:10,902 --> 00:01:12,840
preferred way to restore flow.

18
00:01:13,351 --> 00:01:17,821
Emergency teams can use other pathways
depending on access to treatment, test

19
00:01:17,891 --> 00:01:20,003
results, and the patient’s condition.

20
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:25,402
In the catheter laboratory, the team uses
contrast and moving X-rays to find the

21
00:01:25,437 --> 00:01:29,651
blockage. Here, contrast stops at the
affected spot.

22
00:01:30,255 --> 00:01:34,539
The interventional cardiologist identifies
the artery responsible for this heart

23
00:01:34,574 --> 00:01:36,954
attack and plans how to reopen it.

24
00:01:37,395 --> 00:01:42,701
The treatment is called percutaneous
coronary intervention, or PCI.

25
00:01:43,142 --> 00:01:46,149
A thin guidewire can pass beyond the
blockage.

26
00:01:46,428 --> 00:01:51,281
A balloon and, when appropriate, a stent
are used to reopen and support the treated

27
00:01:51,339 --> 00:01:56,052
segment. The team then checks blood flow
and looks for problems before the

28
00:01:56,099 --> 00:01:57,318
procedure ends.

29
00:01:58,015 --> 00:02:02,461
After the emergency treatment, the team
monitors recovery and explains what the

30
00:02:02,508 --> 00:02:07,372
patient’s tests showed. Ongoing care can
include medicines and

31
00:02:07,453 --> 00:02:12,713
cardiac rehabilitation. Some patients need
a further assessment of other

32
00:02:12,794 --> 00:02:17,751
narrowed arteries. The team discusses the
follow-up plan and what to do if

33
00:02:17,809 --> 00:02:19,272
symptoms return.
